body_text:
## Bevy version and features

- Bevy 0.19.1 (crates.io), `default-features = false, features = ["2d", "3d", "ui"]`

## Relevant system information

- Rust 1.95.0, Windows 11 (10.0.26200), Vulkan
- `AdapterInfo { name: "AMD Radeon(TM) Graphics", vendor: 4098, device: 5708, device_type: IntegratedGpu, driver: "AMD proprietary driver", driver_info: "22.20.44", backend: Vulkan }` (Ryzen 5 5500U, Vega 7)
- Log: `GPU clustering is supported on this device.`

## What you did

Two cameras render the same world, each restricted to its own `RenderLayers`, and each layer has its own point light:

- A grey floor is on layers 0 and 1.
- A red `PointLight` at x = -2.5 is on layer 0 only; a blue `PointLight` at x = +2.5 is on layer 1 only.
- Camera A renders layer 0 and camera B renders layer 1, each into its own 320x180 image (no ambient, EV100 0, tonemapping off).

Each image is read back at frame 30 and the average RGB of its left half (under the red light) and right half (under the blue light) is printed.

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[dependencies]
bevy = { version = "0.19.1", default-features = false, features = ["2d", "3d", "ui"] }
```

`src/main.rs`:

```rust
//! Point lights ignore camera `RenderLayers` when GPU light clustering is on (Bevy 0.19.1).
//!
//!     cargo run                     # default: GPU clustering where supported
//!     CPU_CLUSTERING=1 cargo run    # GlobalClusterSettings::gpu_clustering = None

use bevy::camera::visibility::RenderLayers;
use bevy::camera::{Exposure, RenderTarget};
use bevy::core_pipeline::tonemapping::{DebandDither, Tonemapping};
use bevy::light::cluster::GlobalClusterSettings;
use bevy::prelude::*;
use bevy::render::RenderPlugin;
use bevy::render::render_resource::TextureFormat;
use bevy::render::view::screenshot::{Screenshot, ScreenshotCaptured};

#[derive(Resource, Default)]
struct Targets(Vec<(&'static str, Handle<Image>)>);

#[derive(Resource, Default)]
struct Captured(usize);

fn main() {
    App::new()
        // Synchronous pipeline compilation, so the frame read back is fully drawn.
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins.set(RenderPlugin { synchronous_pipeline_compilation: true, ..default() }))
        .init_resource::<Targets>()
        .init_resource::<Captured>()
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .add_systems(Update, (cpu_clustering_if_asked, capture).chain())
        .run();
}

fn setup(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut images: ResMut<Assets<Image>>,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
    mut targets: ResMut<Targets>,
) {
    commands.insert_resource(GlobalAmbientLight { brightness: 0.0, ..default() });

    // Floor visible to both cameras.
    commands.spawn((
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Plane3d::default().mesh().size(12.0, 12.0))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
            base_color: Color::srgb(0.6, 0.6, 0.6),
            perceptual_roughness: 1.0,
            ..default()
        })),
        RenderLayers::from_layers(&[0, 1]),
    ));

    // Red light on layer 0 only, blue light on layer 1 only.
    for (x, color, layer) in [(-2.5, Color::srgb(1.0, 0.0, 0.0), 0), (2.5, Color::srgb(0.0, 0.0, 1.0), 1)] {
        commands.spawn((
            PointLight { intensity: 20.0, range: 6.0, color, shadow_maps_enabled: false, ..default() },
            Transform::from_xyz(x, 1.0, 0.0),
            RenderLayers::layer(layer),
        ));
    }

    // One camera per layer, each rendering into its own image.
    for (name, layer, order) in [("camera A (layer 0)", 0, -2), ("camera B (layer 1)", 1, -1)] {
        let target = images.add(Image::new_target_texture(320, 180, TextureFormat::Rgba8UnormSrgb, None));
        commands.spawn((
            Camera3d::default(),
            Camera { order, ..default() },
            RenderTarget::Image(target.clone().into()),
            Transform::from_xyz(0.0, 9.0, 9.0).looking_at(Vec3::ZERO, Vec3::Y),
            Msaa::Off,
            Tonemapping::None,
            DebandDither::Disabled,
            Exposure { ev100: 0.0 },
            RenderLayers::layer(layer),
        ));
        targets.0.push((name, target));
    }
}

fn cpu_clustering_if_asked(settings: Option<ResMut<GlobalClusterSettings>>, mut logged: Local<bool>) {
    let Some(mut settings) = settings else { return };
    if std::env::var("CPU_CLUSTERING").is_ok() && settings.gpu_clustering.is_some() {
        settings.gpu_clustering = None;
    }
    if !*logged {
        *logged = true;
        info!("GPU clustering: {}", settings.gpu_clustering.is_some());
    }
}

fn capture(mut commands: Commands, targets: Res<Targets>, mut frame: Local<u32>) {
    *frame += 1;
    if *frame != 30 {
        return;
    }
    for (name, target) in targets.0.clone() {
        commands.spawn(Screenshot::image(target)).observe(
            move |ev: On<ScreenshotCaptured>, mut captured: ResMut<Captured>, mut exit: MessageWriter<AppExit>| {
                let img = ev.image.clone().try_into_dynamic().unwrap().to_rgba8();
                let (w, h) = img.dimensions();
                let mut sums = [[0u64; 3]; 2];
                for (x, _, p) in img.enumerate_pixels() {
                    let side = usize::from(x >= w / 2);
                    for c in 0..3 {
                        sums[side][c] += u64::from(p[c]);
                    }
                }
                let n = u64::from(w / 2 * h);
                let avg = |s: [u64; 3]| [s[0] / n, s[1] / n, s[2] / n];
                println!("{name}: left (red light) avg RGB {:?}, right (blue light) avg RGB {:?}", avg(sums[0]), avg(sums[1]));
                captured.0 += 1;
                if captured.0 == 2 {
                    exit.write(AppExit::Success);
                }
            },
        );
    }
}
```

## What went wrong

With GPU clustering (the default here), **each camera is lit by both point lights**. With `GlobalClusterSettings::gpu_clustering = None` (`CPU_CLUSTERING=1 cargo run`), each camera shows only the light on its own layer, which is what I expected.

Output (two runs each, identical):

| Clustering | Camera | Left half (red light) | Right half (blue light) |
|---|---|---|---|
| GPU (default) | A, layer 0 | [27, 19, 21] | **[19, 19, 29]**: the layer-1 blue light shows |
| GPU (default) | B, layer 1 | **[27, 19, 21]**: the layer-0 red light shows | [19, 19, 29] |
| CPU | A, layer 0 | [27, 19, 20] | [19, 19, 20] |
| CPU | B, layer 1 | [19, 19, 21] | [19, 19, 29] |

## Additional information

- Only point lights were tested; a `DirectionalLight` on layers 0+1 behaved as expected in our larger harness. Spot lights go through the same clustered path but were not tested.
- From reading the 0.19.1 source: the CPU assignment in `bevy_light/src/cluster/assign.rs` filters each clusterable object with `view_layers.intersects(&clusterable_object.render_layers)`, but only runs when `gpu_clustering.is_none()`. The GPU path in `bevy_pbr/src/cluster/gpu.rs` builds one global clustered-light list for all views, and I could not find a render-layer test in it or in the cluster shaders. GPU clustering arrived in #23036, so this may be a regression from 0.18 (not tested).
- Related but different: #23264 (mesh-vs-light layer filtering with one camera) and its PR #23925, which filters by mesh ∩ light layers. Here the floor is on both layers, so that would not seem to help; it is the camera's layers that should exclude the light.
- Workaround: `GlobalClusterSettings::gpu_clustering = None`.
- Use case: an A/B view rendering two look profiles side by side from one world, each camera with its own lights.

