## Bevy version

0.20.0-rc.1 (wgpu / naga 30.0.1)

## Relevant system information

- GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 Super with Max-Q Design, driver 570.181, Vulkan, Linux.
- Not reproducible on Mesa llvmpipe 21.2.6 or Intel UHD Graphics (Mesa 21.2.6) on the same machine.

## What you did

Rendered meshes whose `StandardMaterial` has `double_sided: true` (including glTF materials exported as double-sided), lit by a directional light.

## What went wrong

On the NVIDIA driver, front faces of double-sided materials get a flipped normal: they are lit as if seen from the back (a closed glTF character exported as double-sided looks flat, receiving mostly ambient light; shadow lookups use the wrong normal offset).

`pbr_input_from_vertex_output` calls `winding_corrected_front_facing(pbr_input.flags, is_front)`, which returns `is_front == positive_determinant`. On this driver, an equality where one operand comes from `@builtin(front_facing)` evaluates to `false` when both operands are `true`, so `is_logically_front` is false for front faces and `prepare_world_normal` flips the normal.

Minimal repro (plain wgpu, one full-screen counter-clockwise triangle, i.e. front-facing; the uniform holds `a = 1, b = 1`):

```wgsl
struct U { a: u32, b: u32 }
@group(0) @binding(0) var<uniform> u: U;

@fragment
fn fragment(@builtin(front_facing) front: bool) -> @location(0) vec4<f32> {
    let x = u.a != 0u;
    let y = u.b != 0u;
    return vec4(
        select(0.0, 1.0, x == y),        // 1 on all three GPUs
        select(0.0, 1.0, front == true), // 0 on NVIDIA 570.181, 1 on llvmpipe and Intel
        select(0.0, 1.0, front == x),    // 0 on NVIDIA 570.181, 1 on llvmpipe and Intel
        select(0.0, 1.0, front != !x),   // 1 on all three GPUs
    );
}
```

Only `==` with the front-facing value fails; `!=`, `select` and equalities between other booleans are correct. The SPIR-V generated by naga looks correct (`OpLogicalEqual %bool %front %x`), so this seems to be a driver issue (the `FrontFacing` input apparently holds a non-canonical `true`), but it affects every double-sided `StandardMaterial` for Bevy users on this driver.

## Suggested fix

Avoid comparing the front-facing boolean with `==` in `pbr_functions.wesl`; this is equivalent:

```wgsl
fn winding_corrected_front_facing(mesh_flags: u32, is_front: bool) -> bool {
    let positive_determinant = (mesh_flags & MESH_FLAGS_SIGN_DETERMINANT_MODEL_3X3_BIT) != 0u;
    return select(!is_front, is_front, positive_determinant);
}
```

We apply exactly this change at runtime in our game (by patching the `bevy_pbr::render::pbr_functions` shader asset once it is loaded) and double-sided meshes are lit correctly again on this driver, with no change on the other GPUs. A more general workaround could live in naga's SPIR-V backend (canonicalizing the `FrontFacing` builtin, e.g. through an `OpSelect`), which would also protect user shaders.

