## Bevy version and features

- Bevy 0.20
- `bevy = { version = "0.20", default-features = false, features = ["2d", "ui", "bevy_ui_widgets", "dynamic_linking"] }`
- Windows 10 and Windows 11

## What you did
In my game, I create some entities with sprites in one game state and store them in a DynamicWorld without their sprites to be loaded later in another game state. The original entities are despawned after storing them in the DynamicWorld.

After updating to bevy 0.20, I noticed that the sprites of the previous entities are still being rendered after loading the world.

I was able to recreate this scenario in a small example:

```rust
use bevy::prelude::*;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .add_systems(Update, (log_ocean_sprites, switch))
        .run();
}

#[derive(Component, Clone, Default)]
struct Tile;

fn setup(mut commands: Commands) {
    commands.spawn(Camera2d);

    commands.spawn_scene(bsn! {
        Tile
        Sprite {
            image: "ocean.png"
        }
        Transform::from_translation(Vec3::new(0.0, 0.0, 5.0))
    });
}

fn log_ocean_sprites(sprites: Query<&Sprite>) {
    error!("Num sprite entities: {}", sprites.iter().count());

    for sprite in &sprites {
        let handle = &sprite.image;

        if let Some(path) = handle.path() {
            let string_path = path.to_string();
            if string_path.contains("ocean") {
                error!("Found entity with ocean image");
                return;
            }
        }
    }
}
fn switch(
    mut commands: Commands,
    input: Res<ButtonInput<KeyCode>>,
) {
    if input.just_pressed(KeyCode::Space) {
        // Create a new dynamic world for the current tiles
        commands.run_system_cached(create_new_world);
        // Despawn the current tiles
        commands.run_system_cached(despawn_tiles);
        // Spawn the new world back into the main world
        commands.run_system_cached(spawn_new_world);
        // Spawn new tiles with different textures
        commands.run_system_cached(spawn_new_tiles);
    }
}

#[derive(Resource)]
struct NewWorld(Handle<DynamicWorld>);

fn create_new_world(world: &mut World) {
    let tiles = world
        .query_filtered::<Entity, With<Tile>>()
        .iter(world)
        .collect::<Vec<_>>();

    let new_world =
        DynamicWorldBuilder::from_world(world, &world.resource::<AppTypeRegistry>().read())
            .deny_all_resources()
            .deny_component::<Sprite>()
            .extract_entities(tiles.into_iter())
            .build();

    let mut assets = world.get_resource_mut::<Assets<DynamicWorld>>().unwrap();

    let world_handle = assets.add(new_world);

    world.insert_resource(NewWorld(world_handle));
}

fn despawn_tiles(
    mut commands: Commands,
    tiles: Query<Entity, With<Tile>>,
) {
    for tile in &tiles {
        commands.entity(tile).despawn();
    }
}

fn spawn_new_world(world: &mut World) {
    let new_world = world.get_resource::<NewWorld>().unwrap();

    let mut scene_spawner = WorldInstanceSpawner::default();

    scene_spawner
        .spawn_dynamic_sync(world, new_world.0.id())
        .unwrap();
}

fn spawn_new_tiles(mut commands: Commands) {
    commands.spawn_scene(bsn! {
        Tile
        Sprite {
            image: "sand.png"
        }
        Transform::from_translation(Vec3::new(32.0, 0.0, 0.0))
    });
}
```

This app creates a tile with the image "ocean.png". When I hit space, the following steps are executed in order:
1. Store all tiles without their sprites in a new, dynamic world
2. Despawn all tiles
3. Spawn the tiles back in using the dynamic world handle created previously
4. Spawn in a new tile with a different image ("sand.png")

I also added a system which logs every frame the amount of entities with the `Sprite` component and if the sprite refers to ocean.png.

Before hitting space, only the ocean is shown and the log system tells that only the ocean sprite exists

<img width="354" height="253" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/978ea0f1-d1c8-4d02-9699-37ab24cea24f" />

```
2026-10-11T11:27:46.095625Z ERROR bevy_debug: Num sprite entities: 1
2026-10-11T11:27:46.095684Z ERROR bevy_debug: Found entity with ocean image
```

After hitting space, ocean and sand are rendered, but only one `Sprite` exists in the game:

<img width="330" height="262" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/fc1380ce-1c45-4fdb-98b5-8c9d0c3806c6" />

```
2026-10-11T11:30:28.296618Z ERROR bevy_debug: Num sprite entities: 1
2026-10-11T11:30:28.313640Z ERROR bevy_debug: Num sprite entities: 1
2026-10-11T11:30:28.329718Z ERROR bevy_debug: Num sprite entities: 1
```

## What went wrong

I expect only the desert to be rendered. When switching back to bevy 0.19, the problem disappears.

## Additional Information
In my actual game (which I don't want to share here), I also noticed the following things:
- When I move my camera, these old textures disappear when being close to the edge of the visible area, and reappear when hovering back over them.
- I have a door in my game where I change the texture when it opens or closes (something like `sprite.texture = asset_server.load(...)`. For a split second, I see a different texture, like my player character or a chest. Only one of the 2 textures is loaded at a time, so it must be loaded again after switching. I expect to see nothing in this time frame, but some random texture is used instead.

