Running a system that does a lot of work or stalls causes time.delta_secs to be 1 frame delayed, but only with the multi_threading feature enabled. Tested on the 0.20 release, below is a minimal test case
```
use std::{thread, time::Duration};
use bevy::{diagnostic::FrameCount, input::common_conditions::input_just_pressed, prelude::*};


fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
        .add_systems(Update, test_lag.run_if(input_just_pressed(KeyCode::KeyE)))
        .add_systems(Update, debug_lag)
        .run();
}
fn test_lag(frame: Res<FrameCount>, mut commands: Commands) {
    println!("test_lag frame {}", frame.0);
    thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(1000));
}


fn debug_lag(time: Res<Time>, frame_count: Res<FrameCount>) {
    println!("debug_lag frame {}", frame_count.0);
    if time.delta_secs() > 0.2 {
        println!(
            "lag on frame {}, took {:3}",
            frame_count.0,
            time.delta_secs()
        );
        panic!();
    }
}
```

without multi_threading, we get the expected output:
``` 
debug_lag frame 353
test_lag frame 353
debug_lag frame 354
lag on frame 354, took 0.25
```
However, adding multi_threading causes delta_secs to not update until a frame later.
```
[dependencies]
bevy = {version = "0.20", default-features = false, features = [ "bevy_core_pipeline", "bevy_window", "bevy_winit", "multi_threaded", "wayland"]}
```
```
debug_lag frame 674
test_lag frame 674
debug_lag frame 675
debug_lag frame 676
lag on frame 676, took 0.25
```
In my own program I tested this as well
```
fn debug_lag(time: Res<Time>, frame_count: Res<FrameCount>, now: Res<Now>) {
    dbg!(frame_count.0);
    if time.delta_secs() > 0.24 {
        dbg!(time.delta_secs());
    }
}

