## Bevy version and features

- Discovered at v0.19.1
- Reproduced at v0.20 and main at c8d199b19

## Relevant system information

Windows 11, Vulkan backend

```
AdapterInfo { name: "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090", vendor: 4318, device: 11141, device_type: DiscreteGpu, device_pci_bus_id: "0000:01:00.0", driver: "NVIDIA", driver_info: "617.42", backend: Vulkan, subgroup_min_size: 32, subgroup_max_size: 32, transient_saves_memory: Some(false), limit_bucket: None }
```

## What you did

My game spawns many meshes, many get hidden by terrain. So I try to leverage things like `OcclusionCulling` to avoid rendering what I don't need to. I was doing some graphics profiling (with RenderDoc), and I was finding more meshes were drawn that expected. The RenderDoc capture found the depth pyramid's last column and row were zero at every mip level.

## What went wrong

Not enough meshes are being culled.

## Repro Example

I made a simple repro that spawns 66 cubes. You can then press Space to make a wall visible, which is in front of all the cubes, thus all cubes should be culled. Every second, it uses a diagnostic from `crates/bevy_render/src/diagnostic/internal.rs` to check the number of vertex shader invocations.

Without the wall, it prints 66 cubes are drawn (expected). With the wall, it prints 11 cubes are still drawn (it accounts for wall existing). When enabling `Msaa::Sample4` (via M key), the numbers go from 66 with no wall, to 29 with wall (it gets worse). In a stress test in my actual game, I found that 2,800 of 5,100 meshes were incorrectly kept because of this bug.

<details>
<summary>Repro code</summary>

```rust
use std::time::Duration;

use bevy::{
    core_pipeline::prepass::DepthPrepass,
    diagnostic::{DiagnosticPath, DiagnosticsStore},
    prelude::*,
    render::{diagnostic::RenderDiagnosticsPlugin, occlusion_culling::OcclusionCulling},
    time::common_conditions::on_timer,
};

const INVOCATIONS: DiagnosticPath =
    DiagnosticPath::const_new("render/main_opaque_pass_3d/vertex_shader_invocations");
/// The cubes and the wall are `Cuboid`s: 24 vertex shader invocations each.
const CUBOID_VERTICES: f64 = 24.0;

#[derive(Component)]
struct Wall;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins((DefaultPlugins, RenderDiagnosticsPlugin))
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .add_systems(
            Update,
            (toggle, report.run_if(on_timer(Duration::from_secs(1)))),
        )
        .run();
}

fn setup(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    commands.spawn((
        Wall,
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(100.0, 100.0, 0.1))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(Color::srgb(0.3, 0.3, 0.35))),
        Visibility::Hidden,
    ));
    // 11 x 6 cubes behind the wall, all fully inside the view.
    let cube = meshes.add(Cuboid::from_length(1.0));
    let material = materials.add(Color::srgb(1.0, 0.5, 0.1));
    for x in (-10..=10).step_by(2) {
        for y in (-5..=5).step_by(2) {
            commands.spawn((
                Mesh3d(cube.clone()),
                MeshMaterial3d(material.clone()),
                Transform::from_xyz(x as f32, y as f32, -5.0),
            ));
        }
    }
    commands.spawn((
        DirectionalLight::default(),
        Transform::from_xyz(1.0, 2.0, 3.0).looking_at(Vec3::ZERO, Vec3::Y),
    ));
    commands.spawn((
        Camera3d::default(),
        Msaa::Off,
        DepthPrepass,
        OcclusionCulling,
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, 0.0, 10.0).looking_at(Vec3::ZERO, Vec3::Y),
    ));
}

fn toggle(
    keys: Res<ButtonInput<KeyCode>>,
    mut wall: Single<&mut Visibility, With<Wall>>,
    mut msaa: Single<&mut Msaa>,
) {
    if keys.just_pressed(KeyCode::Space) {
        wall.toggle_visible_hidden();
    }
    if keys.just_pressed(KeyCode::KeyM) {
        **msaa = if **msaa == Msaa::Off {
            Msaa::Sample4
        } else {
            Msaa::Off
        };
    }
}

fn report(
    diagnostics: Res<DiagnosticsStore>,
    wall: Single<&Visibility, With<Wall>>,
    msaa: Single<&Msaa>,
) {
    let Some(invocations) = diagnostics.get(&INVOCATIONS).and_then(|d| d.value()) else {
        return;
    };
    let wall_up = **wall != Visibility::Hidden;
    let cubes = invocations / CUBOID_VERTICES - if wall_up { 1.0 } else { 0.0 };
    info!(
        "{:?}, wall up: {wall_up}: {cubes} of 66 cubes drawn",
        **msaa
    );
}
```
</details>

## Additional Information

I think I was able to find two bugs - one only happens with MSAA enabled, the other happens in with and without MSAA.

1.  MSAA depth downsample reads past the edge. In `downsample_depth.wesl`, `load_mip_0_single_sample` and `load_mip_0_meshlet` do this:

```wgsl
let st0 = vec2<u32>(floor(st - 0.5));
let st1 = st0 + 1u;
```

This seems to be a out-of-bounds read whenever `st` is the last texel, since it blindly read `+ 1u`. From checking a WGSL reference (https://www.w3.org/TR/WGSL/#textureload), the following `textureLoad` will return a 0 for an invalid depth texture read, which prevents culling.

The non-MSAA path uses `textureGather` with a clamp-to-edge sampler, so it doesn't have this problem.

2. The occlusion test reads past the edge, similar clamping bug to above. In `occlusion_culling.wesl`, `get_occluder_depth` loads `aabb_top_left + 1` without clamping. If the box's top-left texel is the last column or row of the mip it tests against, that load is out of bounds, returns 0, and the mesh is kept.

## AI Disclosure

I'm fairly new to understanding graphics programming, so I used Claude quite a bit to discover this issue, and to learn about how all of this stuff works in general.

