## Bevy version and features

Bevy 0.20 or d1cbc9a5 dev

## What you did

I was migrating old code to 0.20 and was steered towards using `bsn!` instead of bundles, by some third-party library documentation. While doing so, I encountered many cryptic error messages trying to get my components to work right. In particular, I have data which **semantically must not implement `Default`.** After trying several things, it seems that the most effective solution is to use the `~` operator to use my component value as a template value (provided it implements `Clone`).

## What went wrong

`~` doesn’t work if the component doesn’t implement `Unpin` (which may happen if, for example, it contains a `Handle`). This does not make sense, because `Unpin` is not relevant to components or templates. Additionally, the error message claims “This type does not manually implement FromTemplate, and it must”, but in fact implementing `FromTemplate` is not the solution to the problem, implementing `Unpin` is.

Reduced example:

```rust
use std::marker::PhantomPinned;
use bevy::prelude::*;

#[derive(Clone)]
struct SomethingElse {}

// Example of a component that works
#[derive(Clone, Component)]
struct Component1 {
    value: SomethingElse,
}

fn test1(mut commands: Commands) {
    commands.spawn_scene(bsn! {
        ~{Component1 { value: SomethingElse {} }}
    });
}

// Example of a component that fails for no good reason
#[derive(Clone, Component)]
struct Component2 {
    value: PhantomPinned,
}

fn test2(mut commands: Commands) {
    commands.spawn_scene(bsn! {
        ~{Component2 { value: PhantomPinned }}
    });
}

// Example of a component that fails with a misleading error message
#[derive(Clone, Component)]
struct Component3 {
    value: Handle<Image>,
}

fn test3(handle: Handle<Image>, mut commands: Commands) {
    commands.spawn_scene(bsn! {
        ~{Component3 { value: handle }}
    });
}
```

`test1` compiles, but `test2` and `test3` do not. They should either all succeed, or help me find what I should be writing instead.

