## Bevy version

0.20.0, and current `main` (checked October 21, 2026: `crates/bevy_input_focus/src/tab_navigation.rs`).

## What you did

Built a UI where part of the tree is hidden with `Display::None` (a collapsed section, an inactive tab panel, a closed toolbar), with focusable controls inside it, then pressed Tab.
```rust
use bevy::prelude::*;
use bevy::input_focus::tab_navigation::{TabGroup, TabIndex, TabNavigationPlugin};
use bevy::ui_widgets::Button;

fn main() {
    App::new()
        .add_plugins((DefaultPlugins, TabNavigationPlugin))
        .add_systems(Startup, setup)
        .add_systems(Update, log_focus)
        .run();
}

fn setup(mut commands: Commands) {
    commands.spawn(Camera2d);
    commands
        .spawn((Node::default(), TabGroup::new(0)))
        .with_children(|root| {
            root.spawn((Button, TabIndex(0), Text::new("Visible A")));
            // A collapsed section: not drawn, takes no space.
            root.spawn(Node { display: Display::None, ..default() })
                .with_child((Button, TabIndex(0), Text::new("Hidden")));
            root.spawn((Button, TabIndex(0), Text::new("Visible B")));
        });
}

//press tab to move focus between the buttons, and see the log output
fn log_focus(focus: Res<bevy::input_focus::InputFocus>, names: Query<&Text>) {
    if focus.is_changed() {
        if let Some(text) = focus.get().and_then(|e| names.get(e).ok()) {
            info!("focus: {}", text.0);
        }
    }
}
```
## What went wrong

Tab goes Visible A → Hidden → Visible B. Focus lands on a button that isn't drawn, so the user can't see where focus went, and Enter or Space can then press a control they can't see. I expected Tab to go straight from Visible A to Visible B, the way browsers skip `display: none`.

## Additional information

`TabNavigation::gather_focusable` skips a subtree only when its `InheritedVisibility` is false (#24769, #24968). `Display::None` removes a node from layout and rendering, but it doesn't change visibility, so these subtrees are still gathered. Non-modal tab groups are gathered globally, so a `TabGroup` nested inside a `Display::None` subtree is reached too.

The usual workaround is to set `Visibility::Hidden` alongside `Display::None` on every hidden part. That's easy to forget, and a library can't always do it, because the app may own that `Visibility`.

`bevy_input_focus` doesn't depend on `bevy_ui`, so it can't check `Node::display` directly. Some possible fixes:

1. Have `bevy_ui` treat `Display::None` as hidden when it propagates visibility (it already knows these nodes aren't rendered).
2. Let `TabNavigation` take a filter, or a marker component that `bevy_ui` keeps on `Display::None` subtrees, that `gather_focusable` skips like a hidden entity.
3. Have `bevy_ui` register its own focusable-gathering rule, for example as part of the focusability work in #25675.

Related: focus that is already on an entity stays there when an ancestor becomes `Display::None`, so key presses keep reaching a control that isn't drawn. Browsers drop focus in that case. That may deserve its own issue.

