## What happens

While a config is loaded (`caddy reload`, `POST /load`), some clients on **new**
HTTP/1.x connections get the connection closed (FIN) without any response byte.
Python reports `RemoteDisconnected('Remote end closed connection without response')`,
Go reports `unexpected EOF`. The request is never logged and never reaches a handler.
These are fresh connections, so clients (and proxies in front of Caddy) don't
retry them, the way they would for a reused keep-alive connection.

We measured it on Linux 6.8 with Caddy 2.11.7 (official binary), Go 1.27.2, and
`net.ipv4.tcp_migrate_req=1`, so the kernel resets almost nothing. Runs of 150 back-to-back
reloads under load closed new connections unanswered: 7,787 of 516,398 requests through `caddy reload`, and 7,496 of 168,304 through `/load` (10 runs each).
On Caddy master (af7620d) the test below fails the same way.

## Why

`modules/caddyhttp/app.go`, `App.stop` → `stopServer` calls
`server.server.Shutdown(ctx)` directly (app.go:791 in v2.11.7, :792 on master).
In net/http (Go 1.27.2, `src/net/http/server.go`):

1. `Server.Shutdown` sets `inShutdown` (server.go:3273) *before* it closes the
   listeners (`closeListenersLocked`, server.go:3279). The accept loop
   (`Serve`, server.go:3574-3604) keeps accepting in between, and each
   connection it accepts is served.
2. A connection in `StateNew` (accepted, first request not read yet) isn't
   closed by Shutdown, which waits up to 5s for it (`closeIdleConns`,
   server.go:3335, golang/go#22682). But as soon as `conn.serve` has read its
   request, it checks `if c.server.shuttingDown() { return }` (server.go:2080)
   and closes the connection without writing a response. That check was added
   for golang/go#33313.

So any connection the old server accepted but hadn't read when Shutdown began,
or that it accepted between steps 1 and 2, is closed unanswered. A loaded
client or a distant one sends its request a few ms after connecting, so the
window covers real traffic. In our traces the closed requests had started up
to 28 ms before the old server's Shutdown began, and up to 1.5 ms after.

Caddy already closes its HTTP/3 listeners before `h3server.Shutdown`
(app.go:808-815), but not its HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 listeners.

Kept-alive connections aren't changed by this. A request in flight finishes and
gets `Connection: close`. A request racing an idle keep-alive connection's close
is the usual HTTP/1.1 keep-alive race, and clients retry those.

## Repro

The test file below (`modules/caddyhttp/acceptgate_test.go`, package caddyhttp) runs against unpatched master:

- `TestReloadAnswersConnectionAcceptedBeforeIt`: dial, wait 100 ms (accepted),
  `caddy.Load` a new config, wait 100 ms, send `GET /`. Fails every time on master:
  `unexpected EOF`.
- `TestReloadLoopClosesNoConnectionUnanswered`: 60 back-to-back loads, with 16
  clients that each dial, wait 0-20 ms, then send. On master, about 40% of
  connections are closed unanswered (535 of 1312 in one run).

## Proposal

Stop accepting before Shutdown. Close the server's HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 listeners,
wait for the accept loops to return, then wait (bounded by the grace period and
by the same 5s Go gives a `StateNew` connection) until every connection they
accepted has reached the handler, gone idle, or closed. Then call Shutdown.
After that, Shutdown has no request left to read, so nothing gets closed
unanswered. PR to follow.

