body_text:
`View.ForegroundColor`, `View.BackgroundColor`, and `Cursor.Color` are `color.Color` interface values, and the renderer compares them with `!=` in `viewEquals` (cursed_renderer.go:901-915) and in the flush-time color diff loop (cursed_renderer.go:476). Interface comparison panics when the dynamic type is uncomparable, so a program that sets a `color.Color` implementation containing a slice, map, or func crashes on the second flush of an unchanged view.

The panic fires inside the ticker goroutine spawned by `startRenderer` (the `p.renderer.flush(false)` call at tea.go:1462). That goroutine has no `recover`, so `recoverFromPanic`/`recoverFromGoPanic` never see it — the process dies and `shutdown`/`restoreTerminalState` never run.

Minimal repro on v2.1.0 (output written to a file so the emitted sequences can be inspected):

```go
package main

import (
	"image/color"
	"os"
	"time"

	tea "charm.land/bubbletea/v2"
)

// color.Color implementation whose dynamic type is uncomparable.
type badColor struct {
	c   color.RGBA
	pad []int
}

func (b badColor) RGBA() (r, g, bl, a uint32) { return b.c.RGBA() }

type model struct{}

type tick struct{}

func (model) Init() tea.Cmd {
	return tea.Tick(400*time.Millisecond, func(time.Time) tea.Msg { return tick{} })
}

func (m model) Update(msg tea.Msg) (tea.Model, tea.Cmd) {
	if _, ok := msg.(tick); ok {
		return m, tea.Quit
	}
	return m, nil
}

func (m model) View() tea.View {
	v := tea.NewView("hello")
	v.AltScreen = true
	v.ForegroundColor = badColor{c: color.RGBA{R: 255, A: 255}, pad: []int{1}}
	return v
}

func main() {
	f, err := os.Create("/tmp/btea-out.bin")
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	defer f.Close()

	p := tea.NewProgram(model{},
		tea.WithInput(nil),
		tea.WithOutput(f),
		tea.WithWindowSize(80, 24),
		tea.WithoutSignals(),
	)
	if _, err := p.Run(); err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
}
```

Observed: `panic: runtime error: comparing uncomparable type main.badColor`, stack going `viewEquals` (cursed_renderer.go:901) <- `flush` (cursed_renderer.go:326) <- `startRenderer.func1` (tea.go:1462), exit code 2. The captured output ends right after `\x1b[?1049h\x1b[?25l...` — alt-screen entered, cursor hidden, bracketed paste enabled — and none of the teardown sequences are ever emitted, so the terminal is left in that state. With a comparable color (`color.RGBA`) the same program exits cleanly.

